{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan","id":395621,"title":"Forsaken by Tenochtitlan","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-06T17:05:59-08:00","id":395621,"title":"Forsaken by Tenochtitlan","content":"Hi, this is my first time posting here, though I have been following this forum for a while now. <br /> Anyways, so my group and I finished playing Requiem for Rome and it was pretty fun. So now we want to play an ancient era for werewolf (big werewolf fan here) but unfortunately there aren't any besides the Sumerian chronicle, which my group wasn't interested in. So I began a project of my own consisting the prime time of the Aztec Empire. I discussed my ideas with my friends and they were loving it (thank goodness). I got Shadows of Mexico a while back and I used it for my project but unfortunately it wasn't very helpful since it was mainly for Vampire. I used my decent knowledge of Mesoamerica and some werewolf supplements books but it wasn't enough. So now I postpone the project until W:tF 2ed comes out. But has anyone played a werewolf Mesoamerican game? I would love to hear it, and any ideas you can share.<br /> P.S. I'm also using the changing breed Balam (not W:tA). I'm going through the nWoD changing breed book but its not very helpful either, or the blasphemies. Any ideas?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T02:08:27-08:00","id":396013,"title":"","content":"Dark Eras got a section on Aztec Skinchangers with a sidebar on what the Forsaken are up to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T05:26:54-08:00","id":396161,"title":"","content":"More specifically, Dark Eras <i>will</i> have a section on Aztec Skinchangers. I don't know whether we've had it confirmed that it will have sidebars for Forsaken specifically as yet.<br /> <br /> Cecoyotl, I haven't played a Mesoamerican Werewolf game, I'm afraid, but it certainly seems like a rich setting for the Forsaken. If you're planning on having the game focus on Tenochtitlan itself, there's some interesting directions you could go with the duality between Tlatelolco and Tenochtitlan, perhaps having two feuding city spirits or a single, dual-aspected city spirit reflecting both states.<br /> <br /> The other thing that strikes me, obviously, is the level of spiritual activity around the Aztec practices of sacrifice. The extent to which there are organised spiritual hierarchies feeding off the death and emotion involved, as opposed to simply 'natural' levels of spirit ecology around them, could well determine how the Forsaken have to treat the Hisil around such areas. On the one hand, the Forsaken might see it as their duty to brutally cull and fight back the spirits born from and feeding from sacrifice; alternatively, however, they might decide (or be forced) to integrate themselves into the human structure in order to interact with the spirit courts of blood and death on a more diplomatic level. Add in the likelihood that at least some sacrificial altars would probably become Loci over time and you could actually set the scene for relatively political conflict between Forsaken and Pure over control and influence in the priesthood, with both sides attempting to work within and through human society to sieze the prize at stake (powerful Loci and control over how the spirit courts are deatl with) rather than just duking it out in open violence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T07:20:59-08:00","id":396328,"title":"","content":"Right, because Tenochtitlan and Tlatelolco was its sister city sharing the north of the island with it until one of the Tenocha kings decided/found an excuse to conquer it and bring its bountiful markets (the most active and prosperous in the empire) under his control. Like any conquest I suspect integration was not necessarily smooth. This turbulence would be doubly potent in the Hisil as a combination of competing spirit courts and new spirits spawned by the emotions of the peoples involved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":168,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","user":"Aiden","date":". 02-07-2015, 10:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T07:24:34-08:00","id":396332,"title":"","content":"On another level, the Aztec Empire was more of a loose collection of city states with similar and cultural practices. While tribute was offered to the rules of the empire, they were allowed to mostly govern themselves, so long as they didn't shirk on their payments.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2650-retias","name":"Retias","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"192"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T07:30:39-08:00","id":396343,"title":"","content":"Here is the mundane document I used for my own Tenochtitlan game.<br /> <br /> I gave this to my players so they had a social and historical context for the non-supernatural components of the city.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Tenochtitlan - 1474 CE/8 - Tochtli<br /> Size: About 12 km2 (4.6 mi2)<br /> Population: 203,400 (including Tlateloco)<br /> Emperor: Axayacatl<br /> <br /> Climate: Cool and temperate, with significant rainfall throughout the year. Even the 'dry' months are wet. Winter months see a decent amount of frost by the water.<br /> <br /> Economy: Primarily textiles, stonework, pottery, featherwork, and military supply; it receives great tribute in metalworks and other commodities. Most of its food is supplied through imports of farm product from the mainland, and by chinampas (platforms on the lake surface where food is cultivated).<br /> <br /> It is also a large hub of the slave trade due to its temples, and its incorporation of the markets of its recently-conquered sister city Tlatelolco on the northern part of the island. Many transients come to see the ball games or make pilgrimage to the temples. The Tlatelolco markets sees tens of thousands each day.<br /> <br /> Law Enforcement: Tenochtitlan is the home of the teccalco court where serious crimes are tried from across the empire; crimes involving nobility are tried in the emperor's palace. The Mexica do not have prisons, though its law is enforced by the military.<br /> <br /> Less serious crimes see the offender required to make restitution to the injured party; this can include becoming their slave for a set period of time. First offenses in serious crimes sometimes result in having your home demolished, or your head shaved. After that a repeat offense will see you put to death by anything from being dragged to a sacrificial altar, strangled to death, or stoned on the spot. The nobility receives far less leeway, often ending up on the altars when wrongdoing is proven in any serious crime.<br /> <br /> Notable City Laws<br /> \u2981 Multiple marriage is legal, with a man having more than one wife.<br /> \u2981 Divorce is legal, and the woman gets half of everything while being free to remarry.<br /> \u2981 Commoners cannot openly display wealth in their dress or home decoration.<br /> \u2981 Public drunkness is illegal except during festivals or if you are over the age of 70.<br /> \u2981 Murder (in the sense of non-lawful killing) is by definition illegal.<br /> <br /> Tlateloco: The Mexica Empire's largest market, on the northern side of the island. Previously it held a large amount of sway over the policy of the empire due to Tenochtitlan's reliance on its markets to fuel the economy, but that seems like it will no longer be an issue. It was conquered just last year when Axayacatl deposed its king Moquihuix; an interim military governor, a quauhtlatoani, will be appointed later this year.<br /> <br /> Recent Events and Opinions<br /> \u2981 Axayacatl is either a true sovereign, and worthy successor to Moctezuma who unified the two cities sharing the island and will bring prosperity to the Mexica people (Most of Tenochtitlan), or an imperialist bastard who annexed their home and is going to replace their king with a puppet (Most of Tlatelolco). In either case the Mexica seem to be looking forward to yet another imperial reign full of expansion and plentiful sacrifices for the gods.<br /> \u2981 The sixth version of the Major Temple was just recently completed, and it is glorious. The blood is flowing freely down its steps, and the sun shines brightly on the Mexica for it. A major sacrifice will be performed in a few years time to dedicate it. Praise be to the gods. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T07:37:51-08:00","id":396347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Acrozatarim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396161#post396161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other thing that strikes me, obviously, is the level of spiritual activity around the Aztec practices of sacrifice. The extent to which there are organised spiritual hierarchies feeding off the death and emotion involved, as opposed to simply 'natural' levels of spirit ecology around them, could well determine how the Forsaken have to treat the Hisil around such areas. On the one hand, the Forsaken might see it as their duty to brutally cull and fight back the spirits born from and feeding from sacrifice; alternatively, however, they might decide (or be forced) to integrate themselves into the human structure in order to interact with the spirit courts of blood and death on a more diplomatic level. Add in the likelihood that at least some sacrificial altars would probably become Loci over time and you could actually set the scene for relatively political conflict between Forsaken and Pure over control and influence in the priesthood, with both sides attempting to work within and through human society to sieze the prize at stake (powerful Loci and control over how the spirit courts are deatl with) rather than just duking it out in open violence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Would not this create the Wounds on this sacrificial alters?<br /> <br /> Also, as far as I know, wolves are not popular in MesoAmerica and Aztec would do not have any Wolf related myths. Maybe stick with this and treat all Uratha as foreign to this region, where normally the Balam are local shapechangers culture? Something like I did with Ursara on my Lapland game, were Uratha are conquerors from centuries before. I would see Uratha as naturally not encontered in this place and they are &quot;intruders&quot; in the Land of Great Cats.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T07:44:01-08:00","id":396351,"title":"","content":"The Aztecs do have legends of native shape shifters referred to as Nagual. Their most common forms, according to Wikipedia, were donkeys, turkeys or dogs. Some were of course things like jaguars and pumas.<br /> <br /> So they have a rather diverse set of shape shifting options. Werewolves seem to fit in nicely as a native component.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T08:52:37-08:00","id":396402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396347#post396347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Would not this create the Wounds on this sacrificial alters?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily, but they certainly <i>might</i> end up as Wounds. Preventing them from doing so could well be a significant drive for the Forsaken within Tenochtitlan's society. Ways that come to mind of stopping them becoming Wounds involve the obvious human side of things (stopping them sacrificing - good luck with <i>that </i>- or more likely trying to influence the rites and practices of the priests to ensure that the sacrifices are less about agony and suffering, and more about stirring up emotions other than dread amongst those who see them), ensuring that there is a co-operative spirit court willing to drink up the Essence created to prevent it from pooling and saturating the <i>Hisil</i>, rites to counteract any creeping encroachment of Wounded taint, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, as far as I know, wolves are not popular in MesoAmerica and Aztec would do not have any Wolf related myths. Maybe stick with this and treat all Uratha as foreign to this region, where normally the Balam are local shapechangers culture? Something like I did with Ursara on my Lapland game, were Uratha are conquerors from centuries before. I would see Uratha as naturally not encontered in this place and they are &quot;intruders&quot; in the Land of Great Cats. </div> </div> </div>Well, Xolotl is a fairly obvious one to draw on with heavy canine overtones (his dog-headed aspects etc). Interestingly, his areas of influence touch on the preserves of both the Bone Shadows and Fire-Touched, indicating there could well be a significant struggle between the two Tribes over certain aspects of society at the time. You could even go with a Xolotl-related Lodge that crosses both Tribes, engaged in an internal war between Pure and Forsaken members to claim its spiritual heart and direction.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, I'm generally less than eager about the presence of werewolves being related to ethnicity in some way, especially given the new ways in 2e that Wolf-Blooded can come into existence. Werewolves are consummate shapechangers and a lack of actual wolves isn't really going to stop them taking whatever forms they need to disguise their presence (and those sightings of Urshul etc can easily be rolled into links with Xolotl) while any bugger who sees Gauru is probably dead or rather unhinged and doesn't have any handily available wolves to compare what he saw to.<br /> <br /> Quite often, people have a bit of a habit of going 'but THIS ethnicity/group are different and have their OWN special (shifters/vampires/whatevers). For me, it would probably be more interesting to have the Uratha as being as native to the population as anywhere else - but pitching the Balam or other shapeshifters as the invaders/outsiders/newly formed presence, shoving the Forsaken into serious conflict as these things start appearing and making inroads, harnessing a human culture that already has lots of imagery and symbolism about jaguars that makes it easy for them to sink their claws into it. Hell, with the Blasphemies version of the Balam Colop you could shuffle the timeline and have their actual origin story play out right in front of the eyes of the Forsaken.<br /> <br /> Go with a storyline indicating some sort of shenanigans with certain priests - maybe there are hints that Claimed are involved, or other spirits, enough to draw attention. Maybe on purpose, maybe by mistake, the priests end up creating Balam Colop by feeding some sacred jaguars with human sacrifices or hearts. The Forsaken bust down whatever occult trouble the priests have brewing without realising that the jaguars are the focus of it all (or maybe the jaguars are purely a side-product and aren't even relevant to whatever the seeming 'main plot' was). The jaguars get loose or are forgotten about at first; the Balam Colop start to infiltrate society, or find themselves mystically bound to stalk those who wrong priests of Tezcatlipoca, or just try to figure out who the hell they are and what their purpose is. The killings begin, the Forsaken naturally suspect Claimed or Uratha, and are extremely surprised as they find a new and cunning creature on the loose...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T10:25:44-08:00","id":396474,"title":"","content":"It's kind of interesting to note that, while what they did was pretty brutal, the Spanish were much better at handling the colonization of the South Americas than their counterparts to the north were. It's probably due to them incorporating the native peoples into their society, even at a reduced social status, than the northern tendency to plop down somewhere, push Native American's out like they owned the land, then expand and push them away further.<br /> <br /> The rampancy of disease is something you might want to cover, as well, at least when the Europeans arrive.<br /> <br /> The Blasphemies book has more than a few hints of storytelling stuff for the time of the Aztecs. There's the previously mentioned Balam Colop, the Lodge of Crossed Swords who were among the conquistadors, and the Lodge of Quetzal, who were created much later but possibly something that existed before and almost died out. They imply the People used Anybeast to take the form of a jaguar when they needed to keep the herd from noticing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 02-07-2015, 01:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T15:09:32-08:00","id":396813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396013#post396013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dark Eras got a section on Aztec Skinchangers with a sidebar on what the Forsaken are up to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I heard about it and I was truly excited that Dark Eras is getting an Aztec Empire setting. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Though I think it won't say or focus much on werewolf rather than the Skinchangers' personal experience during that time, which is still good enough for me!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, as far as I know, wolves are not popular in MesoAmerica and Aztec would do not have any Wolf related myths. Maybe stick with this and treat all Uratha as foreign to this region, where normally the Balam are local shapechangers culture? Something like I did with Ursara on my Lapland game, were Uratha are conquerors from centuries before. I would see Uratha as naturally not encontered in this place and they are &quot;intruders&quot; in the Land of Great Cats.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that is sort of true but in mythology and legends theirs a whole lot of stories revolving the the Nahual and there abilities to shapeshift into whatever animal they hold close to or feel like. Plus, if I remember correctly there was one story told and recorded by a Spanish priest named Geronimo de Aguilar saying about shapeshifting wolves in El Tajin (other pyramid ruins in Mexico). And on the other hand Teotihuacan did worshiped or hold the wolf as a sacred animal, which there are bones buried in the pyramid of the moon and Quetzalcoatl. But in W:tF the Uratha are everywhere in the world. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T17:19:11-08:00","id":396970,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aiden</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396343#post396343\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here is the mundane document I used for my own Tenochtitlan game.<br /> <br /> I gave this to my players so they had a social and historical context for the non-supernatural components of the city.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This is awesome dude! Thanks for sharing this, hope you don't mind if I can use it.<br /> <br /> Wow! These are some awesome ideas! Thank you guys! Though let\u2019s see how I\u2019m going to put the Pure in\u2026. As for the Balam I plan on having a tug of war of territory with the Uratha and make there home turf in the Mayan civilization, if I get to that point. Though which book should I use for the Balam? nWoD Changing Breeds or Blasphemies. The spiritual influence and the flow of spirit energy should be very interesting with all of those sacrifices, especially how the main altars are huge locies. But how does the Uratha control it or ward it? Do the Bone Shadows control the flow of energy and influence around the place, trying to balance it? What about the Hunters in Darkness and the Blood Talons? Do they keep the Pure and the Balam in check? And the Storm Lords\u2026will they be the aristocrats or nobles for the Uratha and Aztec society? Such as trying to lead them or control them. The Iron Masters I see them fairly roaming the city and influencing human society. I plan to use the three main cities, Tenochtitlan, Texcoco, Tlacopan, and some of the outskirts of the cities.<br /> I was also thinking of making the Uratha origin story native to Tenochtitlan by mixing the Aztec gods in it. Coyolxauhqui the moon goddess as mother moon and Father Wolf\u2026I\u2019m not sure yet. Though I can see the Pure worshipping Huitzilopochtli for severing Coyolxauhqui. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-07T17:55:02-08:00","id":397008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is awesome dude! Thanks for sharing this, hope you don't mind if I can use it.</div> </div> </div> </div>By all means, other people using it is why I posted it up here!<br /> <br /> Just give credit where it's due.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-08T18:32:14-08:00","id":398282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> nWoD Changing Breeds or Blasphemies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would go with <b>Changing Breeds</b>, as they are giving more coherent expiriance for other shapeshifters than <b>War with the Pure</b> or <b>Blasphemies </b>- the feel that werebeasts are clashing cousins, not totally alien races. Still, I would advise you to look in to our f<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/33067-ancients-awaken-the-old-ones-chronicles-changing-breeds-discussion-brainstorming?p=168731#post168731\">an 2ed update of this book</a>, as mechanics could be weird in it - but at least it diverse, in contrast to the <b>WwtP</b>. If you want, I can look on Balam and make our fan fixing of it here. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The spiritual influence and the flow of spirit energy should be very interesting with all of those sacrifices, especially how the main altars are huge locies. But how does the Uratha control it or ward it? Do the Bone Shadows control the flow of energy and influence around the place, trying to balance it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm also interested, because by the rules of desecration from <b>Inferno</b>, and massive sacrifice altar quickly become the Wound.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about the Hunters in Darkness and the Blood Talons? Do they keep the Pure and the Balam in check?</div> </div> </div> </div>Remember that in 2ed <b>Forsaken</b> the Blood Talons Prey are other werewolves, but the Hunters in Darkness it's the Hosts. I could see other shapeshifter being moot point here, but I would more go with Hunters here - they want purity of their land. Also, other Changing Breeds are very much like Hosts they fight with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And the Storm Lords\u2026will they be the aristocrats or nobles for the Uratha and Aztec society? Such as trying to lead them or control them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe in 1ed <b>Werewolf</b>. In 2ed, as I see it, Storm Lords are examples - they can be nobles, but also spiritual leaders or hunting perfectionists. It's way they have Claimed as their Prey - because it's so much &quot;unideal&quot; from their view of world to let it live.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Iron Masters I see them fairly roaming the city and influencing human society.</div> </div> </div> </div>One again - remember the Prey of the Tribe. Iron Masters fight with organised humans and a problems they can generate for Uratha. I could see Iron Masters now more like secret assassins, integrating with humans, walking inside them and watching - and then silently killing those that could make a threat to Flesh and Spirit. Also, they are first to sense the power groups that work for the doom of both humans and Uratha.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=396970#post396970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was also thinking of making the Uratha origin story native to Tenochtitlan by mixing the Aztec gods in it. Coyolxauhqui the moon goddess as mother moon and Father Wolf\u2026I\u2019m not sure yet. Though I can see the Pure worshipping Huitzilopochtli for severing Coyolxauhqui.</div> </div> </div> </div>With more of changes in vain of 2ed - the Wolf and Moon can have other gender than Father and Mother, respectively. In mine Scandinavian game we have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths#post137256\">Mani, Moon Father, and Angerboda, Mother of Wolves</a>. In Rome the Uratha probably worship<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths?p=272764#post272764\"> Two Mothers, Capitolean Wolf and Luna goddess</a>. Do not stick with &quot;Father Wolf, Mother Luna&quot; on force - spirits can have any gender in myths and local human culture will add the spice to your game. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Also, in the topic other pointed for the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xolotl\" target=\"_blank\">Xolotl</a> as probable Wolf ( as the Dog ) figure in Aztec region. Seeing as he is God of Day Time, I could see some very strained relationship between him and Coyolxauhqui, maybe even some rape overtones, with how brutal Aztec gods were.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-08T19:36:16-08:00","id":398309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=398282#post398282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm also interested, because by the rules of desecration from <b>Inferno</b>, and massive sacrifice altar quickly become the Wound.</div> </div> </div> </div>A sacrificial altar in a culture where sacrifice is a regular part of religious practices without incorporating torture and suffering doesn't strike me as inherently likely to pick up resonance in line with the word &quot;atrocity.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-08T19:51:55-08:00","id":398316,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=398309#post398309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A sacrificial altar in a culture where sacrifice is a regular part of religious practices without incorporating torture and suffering doesn't strike me as inherently likely to pick up resonance in line with the word &quot;atrocity.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Seriously.<br /> <br /> Wounds aren't just about 'sin', they're about <i>violation</i>. You can't have an action be a violation if it's an expected part of life.<br /> <br /> Look, I'm not saying the sacrificial nature of the Aztecs religion isn't messed up, but there's acknowledging a problem with the society and blowing it up to make the Aztecs a culture of pure evil. It wasn't, sorry.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 02-08-2015, 10:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-08T21:34:45-08:00","id":398392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=398309#post398309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A sacrificial altar in a culture where sacrifice is a regular part of religious practices without incorporating torture and suffering doesn't strike me as inherently likely to pick up resonance in line with the word &quot;atrocity.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, so as long as I massively kill people in quick death the Locus is not corrupted. It does have sense if you think about all the sacrifices to Odin and other gods without creating Wound.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-08T21:43:50-08:00","id":398421,"title":"","content":"That is one of those things the Uratha would find motivation in for doing things like acquiring influence among the priesthood. If the rites get out of hand things can go very wrong very fast.<br /> <br /> There are Maeljin out there that wish for just that very thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-09T12:52:06-08:00","id":399200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aiden</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=397008#post397008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> By all means, other people using it is why I posted it up here!<br /> <br /> Just give credit where it's due.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course! Thanks again!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would go with <b>Changing Breeds</b>, as they are giving more coherent expiriance for other shapeshifters than <b>War with the Pure</b> or <b>Blasphemies </b>- the feel that werebeasts are clashing cousins, not totally alien races. Still, I would advise you to look in to our f<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/33067-ancients-awaken-the-old-ones-chronicles-changing-breeds-discussion-brainstorming?p=168731#post168731\">an 2ed update of this book</a>, as mechanics could be weird in it - but at least it diverse, in contrast to the <b>WwtP</b>. If you want, I can look on Balam and make our fan fixing of it here. <img src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I was aiming for something like that. Oh I would like that very much!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One again - remember the Prey of the Tribe. Iron Masters fight with organised humans and a problems they can generate for Uratha. I could see Iron Masters now more like secret assassins, integrating with humans, walking inside them and watching - and then silently killing those that could make a threat to Flesh and Spirit. Also, they are first to sense the power groups that work for the doom of both humans and Uratha.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh jeez\u2026I need to refresh on W:tF 2ed news. I guess I\u2019m too stuck on W:tF 1ed rules and system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">With more of changes in vain of 2ed - the Wolf and Moon can have other gender than Father and Mother, respectively. In mine Scandinavian game we have <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths#post137256\">Mani, Moon Father, and Angerboda, Mother of Wolves</a>. In Rome the Uratha probably worship<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths?p=272764#post272764\"> Two Mothers, Capitolean Wolf and Luna goddess</a>. Do not stick with &quot;Father Wolf, Mother Luna&quot; on force - spirits can have any gender in myths and local human culture will add the spice to your game. <img src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> Also, in the topic other pointed for the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xolotl\" target=\"_blank\">Xolotl</a> as probable Wolf ( as the Dog ) figure in Aztec region. Seeing as he is God of Day Time, I could see some very strained relationship between him and Coyolxauhqui, maybe even some rape overtones, with how brutal Aztec gods were.</div> </div> </div> </div>I realized that part about genders but for story and religion sakes I\u2019m sticking to Coyolxauhqui as mother moon and Xolot as Father Wolf. Also I can totally see Acrozatarim\u2019s idea about Xolot Lodge between Bone Shadow and Fire Touch since Xolot was a god of death and disease, which would be very nice and dramatic for the two. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, so as long as I massively kill people in quick death the Locus is not corrupted. It does have sense if you think about all the sacrifices to Odin and other gods without creating Wound.</div> </div> </div> </div>If I remember correctly most people who were offer to be sacrifice were under powerful hallucinogens so they won\u2019t feel pain or misery. So I guess that\u2019s one way they kept the locies from becoming Wounds. Also in Aztec society it was considered a great honor to be sacrificed to the gods because it was believed that they would be doing the land or the world a favor by replenishing it or rejuvenating it with their own life force to the gods, and if you were a commoner or a slave, then it would boost your family\u2019s or master\u2019s status in society. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T06:33:57-08:00","id":404430,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=399200#post399200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I remember correctly most people who were offer to be sacrifice were under powerful hallucinogens so they won\u2019t feel pain or misery.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've seen a few references to this and most of them seem to talk about <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulque\" target=\"_blank\">pulque</a> which is a fermented drink made from a rather cactus-like plant - once again proving that people will make alcohol out of <i>anything</i>. It seems to have been ritualistically important and I presume the victims who were fed it basically ended up drunk and intoxicated, hence reducing the fear and the pain. It strikes me that a drink like pulque and other such substances, so central to ritual behaviour amongst the human community, would probably also be valued and used by the Uratha for their own rites and sacred practices.<br /> <br /> tl;dr version: Werewolves drunk on magic cactus juice.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T06:58:39-08:00","id":404443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cecoyotl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=399200#post399200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also in Aztec society it was considered a great honor to be sacrificed to the gods because it was believed that they would be doing the land or the world a favor by replenishing it or rejuvenating it with their own life force to the gods, and if you were a commoner or a slave, then it would boost your family\u2019s or master\u2019s status in society.</div> </div> </div> </div>The neighboring nations many of the sacrifices were taken from in the first place may not have felt the same way when their family members were taken and drugged by a more advanced society so they could have their hearts taken out in public to celebrate a new building being finished.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T08:38:57-08:00","id":404500,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=404443#post404443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The neighboring nations many of the sacrifices were taken from in the first place may not have felt the same way when their family members were taken and drugged by a more advanced society so they could have their hearts taken out in public to celebrate a new building being finished.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aztecs sacrificed their own kids, when the captives sacrifices weren't enough (droughts, volcanic activities, etc). And their neighboring nations engaged in the same behaviors. Aztecs simply won more wars.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T11:00:57-08:00","id":404709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=404500#post404500\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Aztecs sacrificed their own kids, when the captives sacrifices weren't enough (droughts, volcanic activities, etc).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And their neighboring nations engaged in the same behaviors. Aztecs simply won more wars. </div> </div> </div>This isn't true. The Chichimecs were moon worshipers who kept to themselves and other peoples of the area had their own belief systems, many of which didn't involve human sacrifice, or if they did, like the Pur\u00e9pecha, it wasn't nearly on such a grand scale. The Aztecs won more wars because they were the main, though not only, ones actively trying to conquer everyone. Some of the nations they kept peaceful alliances with were mostly kept because they bullied the nations into participating in their flower wars so they could get slaves and sacrifices.<br /> <br /> Everyone in Mesoamerica wasn't of the same mind as the Aztecs. Calling them monsters or evil because of their religion is silly, but so is ignoring the fact that they were a very aggressive culture that was very cruel to their enemies, and even their reluctant allies feared them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-13T14:31:24-08:00","id":405109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/395621-forsaken-by-tenochtitlan?p=404443#post404443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The neighboring nations many of the sacrifices were taken from in the first place may not have felt the same way when their family members were taken and drugged by a more advanced society so they could have their hearts taken out in public to celebrate a new building being finished.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, well, that\u2019s true. But what I meant are the citizens of the Aztec Empire.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aztecs sacrificed their own kids, when the captives sacrifices weren't enough (droughts, volcanic activities, etc). And their neighboring nations engaged in the same behaviors. Aztecs simply won more wars.</div> </div> </div> </div>That isn\u2019t wholly true. If you look at the Aztec cosmology they only sacrificed children during the dry season festival of Atlcualo (The Ceasing of Water), which was once a year to bring water to the land. At this time they worshipped the gods of water such as Tlaloc, Tlaloque, and Chalchihuitlicue. They sacrifice children to the water gods because it is believe that there realm or heaven (Tlalocan) was depicted as a place of unending springtime where vegetation never stopped growing and children who have died were sent here to be nurtured.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This isn't true. The Chichimecs were moon worshipers who kept to themselves and other peoples of the area had their own belief systems, many of which didn't involve human sacrifice, or if they did, like the Pur\u00e9pecha, it wasn't nearly on such a grand scale. The Aztecs won more wars because they were the main, though not only, ones actively trying to conquer everyone. Some of the nations they kept peaceful alliances with were mostly kept because they bullied the nations into participating in their flower wars so they could get slaves and sacrifices.<br /> <br /> Everyone in Mesoamerica wasn't of the same mind as the Aztecs. Calling them monsters or evil because of their religion is silly, but so is ignoring the fact that they were a very aggressive culture that was very cruel to their enemies, and even their reluctant allies feared them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, exactly. Sure they sacrifice many people but not to the point of \u201ceveryday\u201d or a super grand scale, as the Spanish priests would like to exaggerate while forcefully trying to convert the Natives to Catholicism.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}